{"questions":[{"question_id":"X7Qpe69FCzAaaTrM","paperTitle":"AIEEE 2006","year":2006,"examDate":2006,"subject":"chemistry","chapter":"thermodynamics","chapterGroup":"physical-chemistry","topic":"first-law-of-thermodynamics","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"an-ideal-gas-is-allowed-to-expand-both-reversibly-and-irreve-2005-marks-4-x7qpe69fczaaatrm.htm","content":"An ideal gas is allowed to expand both reversibly and irreversibly in an isolated system. If T<sub>i</sub> is the\ninitial temperature and T<sub>f</sub> is the final temperature, which of the following statements is correct?","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"(T<sub>f</sub>)<sub>irrev</sub> &gt; (T<sub>f</sub>)<sub>rev</sub>"},{"identifier":"B","content":"(T<sub>f</sub>)<sub>rev</sub> = (T<sub>f</sub>)<sub>irrev</sub>"},{"identifier":"C","content":"T<sub>f</sub> &gt; T<sub>i</sub>\n for reversible process but T<sub>f</sub> = T<sub>i</sub>\n for irreversible process "},{"identifier":"D","content":"T<sub>f</sub> = T<sub>i</sub> for both reversible and irreversible processes "}],"correct_options":["A"],"explanation":"<b>NOTE :</b> In a reversible process the work done is greater than in irreversible process. Hence the heat absorbed in reversible process would be greater than in the latter case. So,\n<br><br><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.156ex\" height=\"0.036ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 0 1837 16\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(167,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(334,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(501,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(668,0)\"></g><g data-mml-no